    "title": "Atlanta Hawks first and second round picks to make introductions Thursday at Hawks HQ - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Donnell Suggs ,\n1 days ago Atlanta Hawks first-round pick in the 2026 NBA Draft, Zuby Ojiofor, is on an electronic billboard outside of State Farm Arena.\nPhoto by Donnell Suggs/The Atlanta Voice The Atlanta Hawks 2026 NBA Draft is done, and three players are expected to meet with local media on Thursday, June 25, at the team’s practice facility.\nThe first round of the 2026 NBA draft took place on Tuesday night. The Atlanta Hawks had two first-round draft picks in this year’s draft. The draft follows a successful 2025-2026 campaign that saw the Hawks not only make the playoffs but also win back a large majority of their fan base, which had seemed alienated after the team traded All-Star guard Trae Young to the Washington Wizards.\nWith their first pick in this year’s draft, the eighth overall, the Atlanta Hawks selected University of Houston guard Kingston Flemings. A 6’4″ guard out of Newport News, Virginia, Flemings was a freshman All-American during his sole collegiate season.\nFlemings (above) spoke to the media via Zoom following his selection. Photo by Donnell Suggs/The Atlanta Voice With the 23rd overall selection, the Hawks selected St. John’s forward, Zuby Ejiofor. During his three seasons at St. John’s, Ejiofor improved his scoring and rebounding averages every season. He was recruited to Kansas after a standout high school career in Garland, Texas.\nWith the team’s only second-round pick, which began in the 57th slot, the team traded up to get former University of North Carolina center Henri Veesar.\nVeesar, a 22-7ear-old seven-footer from Estonia, averaged 17 points, nearly nine rebounds, and two assists per game for the Tar Heels last season.\nFlemings spoke to the media via Zoom half an hour after he was selected on Tuesday. He said he was anxious while waiting for his name to be called and was happy to hear that Atlanta had selected him. “Knowing I’m going to a good organization is exciting to me,” Flemings said.\nFlenings cited Philadelphia 76ers star Tyrese Maxey as a player he models his game after.\nOver the past five years, for example, the Hawks have had successes and failures at the NBA Draft. Players like Jalen Johnson, drafted 20th overall in 2021, and Onyeka Okongwu, drafted sixth overall in 2020, have become starters and key players for the franchise. While the former first overall pick in the 2024 draft, Zaccahrie Risacher, hasn’t lived up to the hype associated with being the top pick in a draft. Last season, Risacher averaged 9.6 points and 3.8 rebounds on 45.5% shooting from the field and was virtually out of head coach Quin Snyder’s rotation during the playoff series against the eventual champion New York Knicks.\nThe post appeared first on .",
    "summary": "1 days ago Atlanta Hawks first-round pick in the 2026 NBA Draft, Zuby Ojiofor, is on an electronic billboard outside of State Farm Arena. The draft follows a successful 2025-2026 campaign that saw the Hawks not only make the playoffs but also win back a large majority of their fan base, which had seemed alienated after the team traded All-Star guard Trae Young to the Washington Wizards. With their first pick in this year’s draft, the eighth overall, the Atlanta Hawks selected University of Houston guard Kingston Flemings.",
